// === PREAMBLE ===
// @trope    In Medias Res
// @category Narrative Devices / Structure
// @source   https://allthetropes.org/wiki/In_Medias_Res
// @domain   Narrative
// @version  1.3
//
// Laconic (per the wiki): from Horace, "into the middle of things" — the story OPENS at a late
// point, after much action has already happened; the beginning is filled in afterward. Homer's
// Iliad opens in the ninth year of the ten-year siege (Older Than Feudalism); the Aeneid does the
// same. On TV it is usually a preamble to a "How We Got Here" flashback. FRONTIER: this is an
// ORDERING trope, not a level-crossing one. It rides Freytag's pyramid and INVERTS the canonical
// `exposition -> rising_action` sequence — the opening scene depicts a mid-arc DramaticBeat
// (rising_action / climax), and the exposition that PRECEDES it in story-time is narrated LATER.
// The backfill is a `past scene {}` — a backward QUERY within ONE canon plane (the beginning
// really IS earlier; it is merely told out of order), NOT the level-axis crossing into an embedded
// <<level>> that Flashback / Frame Story use. (How-We-Got-Here = In Medias Res opening + Flashback
// backfill; the two compose.)


// === IMPORTS & ASSOCIATIONS ===
import "trl/modules/freytags_pyramid.trl"   // exposition / rising_action / climax — the beats it reorders
import "trl/concepts/index.trl"             // the_past, revelation

assoc in_medias_res -> [
  exposition    : "defers the",
  the_past      : "fills in the earlier",
  flashback     : "the backfill is usually a",
  frame_story   : "an ordering cousin, not a level-crosser like the",
  rising_action : "opens already inside the"
]


// === ONTOLOGY ===
// An ordering device: anachronic (told out of sequence) and front-loaded (opens mid-arc).

imply InMediasRes -> [Anachronic, FrontLoaded, Ordering, Narrative]


// === ABSTRACT RULE ===
// Naming rule: when the OPENING event drops us into a mid-arc beat — a character bonded to
// `rising_action` (the rising war, the quarrel) rather than `exposition` — AND the deferred
// beginning is supplied only LATER, reachable as a `past scene {}` backward query that bonds the
// same character to `exposition`, that inverted ordering IS In Medias Res. The opening is tagged
// and the audience is dropped mid-action, left to reconstruct how it came to be. Contrast Flashback:
// there the earlier material sits one LEVEL down (a crossing into <<level>>); here it stays on the
// SAME canon plane, only out of telling-order — a backward query, not a level descent.
rule In_Medias_Res {
  when:
    evt $opening [&Opens]
    char $c -- rising_action
    not ( $c -- exposition )
    past scene $earlier {
      char $c -- exposition
    }
  then:
    $opening [+InMediasRes]
    audience [#Curious(about=how)]
    surface_global(title="Into the Middle of Things", description="We open mid-action; the beginning is filled in later.")
}


// === CONCRETE VIGNETTE: Homer's Iliad — opening in the ninth year of the siege (epic poem, c. 8th c. BCE) ===
// The poem does NOT begin with Paris and Helen or the launching of the thousand ships. It opens
// already deep inside the war — year nine of ten — with the quarrel between Agamemnon and Achilles
// and the wrath that drives the plague through the Achaean camp. The earlier years (the abduction,
// the muster at Aulis, the long siege) are the exposition, and they are supplied LATER, narrated by
// the characters' own recollection — the same plane, told out of order. Two scenes: `the_wrath`
// (the opening, mid-arc) and `before_the_walls` (the earlier years, backfilled).

// ACHILLES — the best of the Achaeans, withdrawn in rage
char achilles [+Protagonist] [#Wrathful]

// AGAMEMNON — overlord of the host, who seized Achilles' prize
char agamemnon [+Authority] [+Antagonist]

// HELEN — the cause of the war, ten years gone
char helen [+LoveInterest]

// EXT. THE ACHAEAN CAMP BEFORE TROY - DAY (the ninth year)
set camp [+Exterior] [+Day]

// EXT. THE WALLS OF TROY - the years of siege already past
set walls [+Exterior]

// ===== the opening — authored FIRST: we are dropped into the ninth year, mid-action =====
// The telling-order is what matters: the mid-arc scene is narrated first. The rule keys on an
// [&Opens] event (scope-block openers can't carry a tag, and `past scene {}` is a rule-only
// backward query — not a vignette block — so the deferred exposition is just an ordinary scene
// written AFTER this one, queried back into by the rule).
scene the_wrath {
  beat 1 {
    evt open [&Opens]   // the poem begins HERE — the middle of the war, not its start
    achilles @ camp
    agamemnon @ camp
    // the poem's first line is already the quarrel — the middle of the war, not its start
    achilles -- rising_action : "The Wrath of Achilles; The Quarrel Over the Prize"
    achilles >< agamemnon : "Sing, Goddess, of the Wrath"
    // In_Medias_Res -> open [+InMediasRes]; audience [#Curious(about=how)]
  }
}

// ===== the deferred exposition — authored LATER though it is EARLIER in story-time =====
// The abduction, the muster at Aulis, the long siege — the beginning, supplied only now, on the
// same canon plane (not a level-crossing flashback, just out of telling-order). The rule's
// `past scene {}` backward query reaches this scene from the opening.
scene before_the_walls {
  beat 1 {
    helen @ walls
    achilles -- exposition : "Ten Years of Siege; Helen Behind the Walls"
  }
}
